      "q": "14. From a well shuffled pack of $${52}$$ cards, black aces and black queens are removed. From the remaining cards a card is drawn at random, find the probability of drawing a king or a queen.\nAns.\nA pack of cards contains $52$ cards. From them, there are only $2$ black ace cards and $2$ black queen cards.\nTherefore, the number of cards left $=52-2-2=48$.\nSo, there are $48$ equally likely cases available.\nAlso, in $48$ cards, there are a total $$4+2=6$$ queens and kings left. Thus, the number of favourable cases is $6$.\nHence, the probability of drawing a king or a queen is $=\\frac{6}{48}=\\frac{1}{6}$.",
